+## Classifying the report (issue #539)
+
+The claims `progress._reports_transient_cause` and `progress._is_main_session_work` depend
+on: which error **kinds** the vendor itself treats as transient, where an HTTP **status**
+reaches the stream, and how a **recovered** session shows itself. No new fixture file: the
+classification cases are built inline in `test_terminal_error_classification.py` (a C4 red
+leg reverts any `fixtures/*.jsonl` a patch adds), and the two pinned death records above
+are replayed there too.
+
+### Observed
+
+* The incident record (`claude_api_error_death.transcript.jsonl`, 2.1.228) carries
+  `"error":"server_error"` on a lost connection, and that leaf exited **1** after it — the
+  kind and the exit this classification retries.
+* The permanent record (`claude_api_error_permanent.transcript.jsonl`, 2.1.222) carries
+  `"error":"model_not_found"` and, in the transcript spelling only, `"apiErrorStatus":404`.
+  Its stream spelling (derived above) has no status field — see the next list.
+
+### Derived (claude-code 2.1.284, the installed binary; the greps below re-verify)
+
+* **The kind enum** — `z(["authentication_failed","oauth_org_not_allowed",
+  "account_on_hold","verification_required","billing_error","rate_limit","overloaded",
+  "invalid_request","model_not_found","server_error","unknown","max_output_tokens",
+  "cloud_credential_error"])`. It rides the main-loop emitter's `error:n.error`.
+* **The vendor's own transient rule** — `function Akr(e){return e.apiErrorIsTransient===!0||
+  e.error==="overloaded"||e.error==="server_error"}`, and the set it retries a sub-agent's
+  API-error termination on: `var rl=new Set(["rate_limit","overloaded","server_error"]);
+  function il(e,s){if(!(e instanceof Ao))return null;if(!e.errorKind||!rl.has(e.errorKind))
+  return null;…` (`Ao` is `AgentApiErrorTerminationError`). The `apiErrorIsTransient` flag
+  never reaches the stream: the wire converter `function _o(e,s){…r={aborted:…,
+  is_api_error_message:…,api_error:…,api_error_params:…,api_error_code:…,…}` does not carry
+  it. Hence `_CLAUDE_TRANSIENT_KINDS = {server_error, overloaded, rate_limit}`.
+* **A lost connection is stamped `server_error`** — ``if(w&&(JF.has(w.code)||J0.has(w.code)))
+  return Qo({content:`${Pa}: Connection to the API was lost (${w.code}). This is usually
+  temporary — try again.`,error:"server_error"})``, with `JF` = ECONNRESET, EPIPE,
+  ConnectionClosed, UND_ERR_SOCKET, ETIMEDOUT, ECONNABORTED, ERR_SOCKET_CLOSED,
+  StreamSuspended, StreamTruncated and `J0` = ECONNREFUSED, ConnectionRefused, ENOTFOUND,
+  ENETUNREACH, ENETDOWN, EHOSTUNREACH, EHOSTDOWN, EAI_AGAIN, FailedToOpenSocket,
+  ERR_PROXY_TUNNEL. A response that never arrives is `error:"server_error",
+  apiError:"no_response"`. The same kind also carries `apiError:"tls_untrusted_ca"` /
+  `"gateway_content_type"`: the vendor's rule keys on the kind alone, so the harness does
+  too (and a TLS failure dies before any work, which #138 already retried).
+* **`unknown` is the mapper's fallback** — `if(e instanceof Pt)return Qo({content:…,
+  error:"unknown"})`, ``if(e instanceof Error)return Qo({content:`${Pa}: ${e.message}`,
+  error:"unknown"})``, and a bare `return Qo({content:Pa,error:"unknown"})`. It is the one
+  kind whose text the harness reads, and only against the categories it retries. Two of
+  the `unknown` emitters add a typed cause — `error:"unknown",apiError:e.reason===
+  "signin_required"?"gateway_signin_required":"gateway_session_expired"` and
+  `error:"unknown",…apiError:"provider_credentials"` — both sign-in / credential stops. The
+  schema says of `api_error`: *"Typed kind of the API error … for consumers that key on the
+  cause instead of the message text … new values are added over time, so treat an unknown
+  value as absent."* The harness goes one step further and treats **any** typed kind on an
+  `unknown` report as "the vendor classified it", so the prose is not read; that only ever
+  withholds a retry, never grants one.
+* **Where an HTTP status reaches the stream** — only on the `result` wrap-up's `success`
+  variant: `variant:{subtype:"success",api_error_status:Ut,…}` with
+  `pt=n.isApiErrorMessage===!0,Ut=n.apiErrorStatus??null` taken from the last main-loop
+  assistant message, and `is_error:pt`. The `error_*` variants are `{subtype:…,errors:ws}`
+  with no status. The SDK schema declares an optional `api_error_status` on the assistant
+  message, but no emitter in 2.1.284 sets it (`_o` above has no such key). The statuses
+  worth another attempt are the API client's own retry rule: `if(e.status===408)return!0;
+  if(e.status===409)return!0;if(e.status===429)return!0;if(e.status>=500)return!0;`.
+* **What "the session carried on" looks like** — the main-loop emitter `PYt` writes
+  `{type:"assistant",…,parent_tool_use_id:null,…,error:n.error,…_o(n),…}` and, for a tool
+  result, `{type:"user",message:n.message,session_id:Y(),parent_tool_use_id:null,…}`. A
+  sub-agent's `user` events carry the Task's `parent_tool_use_id` (the `agent_progress`
+  branch: `case"user":{yield{type:"user",message:g.message,parent_tool_use_id:
+  e.parentToolUseID,…}`). So main-session `assistant` **and** `user` events clear a report
+  (the CLI recovered); a sub-agent's never do.
+
+Not observed: a 2.1.284 stream of a real API-error death. The minified names (`Akr`, `rl`,
+`_o`, `PYt`, `JF`, `J0`) change between builds; grep the literals instead:
+
+```sh
+B="$(readlink -f "$(command -v claude)")"          # e.g. …/versions/2.1.284
+grep -ao 'new Set(\["rate_limit","overloaded","server_error"\])' "$B"
+grep -ao '.\{80\}e.error==="overloaded"||e.error==="server_error".\{0,40\}' "$B"
+grep -ao 'z(\["authentication_failed".\{0,300\}' "$B"
+grep -ao '.\{120\}error:"unknown".\{0,120\}' "$B"
+grep -ao 'if(e.status===408).\{0,120\}' "$B"
+```
+
